\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Order, measures, and metric completion}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Eliminating one coordinate in \ref{fdip-00BA} is the Schur
complement of \ref{fdip-000D}. The eliminated diagonal entry
is positive, and the complement is computed by its reciprocal.
That reciprocal is a field operation. The reflection inequality
in \ref{fdip-00FP} uses the order on the reals, through squares
of integrals and the positive weights #{w_i} and excesses
#{\lambda_i}. The law #{\nu} of \ref{fdip-00BC} is a projective
limit of Borel probabilities. Each factor is the line, which
is second-countable and completely pseudo-metrizable, and each
finite-dimensional law is a probability measure. The completion
of the shorter-arc metric in \ref{fdip-00BE} produces the
circle as an index set. That completion is separate from the
construction of #{\nu}.}

\p{\ref{fdip-00FX} records that the real algebraic numbers are
real-closed and that they contain a Cauchy sequence with no
limit in the set. A limit of a sequence in a metric space is a
different mathematical object from a limit of rewrite rules.
Complete, in \ref{fdip-00DZ}, means a complete set of rewrite
rules in the sense of Gao, Shaikh, and Kissinger.}
